  "fashion": {
    "Overview": "Clothing operated as a form of identification within the subculture. The Stone Island compass patch, buttoned onto the left sleeve, held particular status among members. C.P. Company jackets and caps, built with glass lenses over the brim or hood, were also sought after; pulling the hood or brim down obscured the wearer's face. Napapijri anoraks, recognizable by their Norwegian-flag patch, were commonly worn as outerwear during colder months. For footwear, retro Adidas models such as the Gazelle, Samba, and Campus were worn for their flat soles and low-profile design, which suited them to street confrontations."
  },
